------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6h. BOTTOM LINE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHICH METHOD?
  may1996: lowest RMSE is ordinary kriging (3.537 m); lowest near-cutoff
           misclassification is IDW power 2 (37.9%).
  may2000: lowest RMSE is IDW power 1 (3.551 m); lowest near-cutoff
           misclassification is IDW power 2 (38.2%).

  Kriging beats the nearest-neighbour baseline by 19.2% on RMSE,
  but beats IDW power 2 by only 1.5%. The real gap is between NN and
  everything else; the choice among IDW and kriging is close to a tie.

  RECOMMENDATION: IDW power 2 as the working method -- it is the simplest,
  it is within a few percent of kriging on RMSE, and it has the LOWEST
  near-cutoff misclassification in both vintages, which is the metric the
  design turns on. Keep ordinary kriging alongside it for one reason: it
  is the only method here that emits a per-prediction standard error, and
  that SE is well calibrated (95% coverage of 0.934 and 0.954 -- section
  6f(iv)). A fuzzy RD or a measurement-error correction needs exactly
  that SE, so kriging earns its place as the uncertainty-bearing variant
  rather than as the accuracy winner.

HOW BIG IS THE MISCLASSIFICATION RISK AT 8 m?
  may1996, ordinary kriging: 25.1% of ALL UP wells land on the wrong
    side of 8 m; among the 391 wells whose true depth is within
    +/-2 m of 8 m, 39.1% are misclassified.
  may2000, ordinary kriging: 25.9% of ALL UP wells land on the wrong
    side of 8 m; among the 419 wells whose true depth is within
    +/-2 m of 8 m, 39.6% are misclassified.

  Against the no-information benchmark (36.8% / 36.8%), interpolation
  improves near-cutoff classification by only -1.0 pp and -1.4 pp.

  This is the number that matters, and it is a WARNING, not a detail.
  Wells near the cutoff are exactly the ones an RD relies on, and at 8 m
  interpolated depth is close to uninformative about which side a unit
  falls on. That is severe measurement error in the RUNNING VARIABLE: it
  attenuates the estimate and blurs the discontinuity, and no choice among
  the methods tested repairs it -- they are all within a few points of
  each other and of guessing.

  IMPLICATION FOR E1, stated plainly: an interpolated running variable
  built from this well network will not support a sharp RD at 8 m in UP.
  The realistic options are the spec's own fallbacks (PHASE0_SPEC.md 4.3,
  7): a FUZZY RD that instruments assigned treatment with interpolated
  depth, or a NEAR-WELL SUBSAMPLE where error is smallest -- and section
  6f(ii) shows that even under 5 km the RMSE is still about 2.7-3.2 m,
  against a 8 m cutoff. This is a quantitative reason to treat E1 as high
  risk, and to prefer Sekhri's own validated depth measure if the PI can
  obtain it.

DOES THE 2000 VINTAGE CHANGE THE PICTURE?
  Ordinary kriging RMSE moves by +0.033 m and near-cutoff
  misclassification by +0.5 pp when moving from the 1996 vintage
  to the earliest-May-<=-2000 vintage (1,309 vs 1,223 UP wells).
  READING: the two vintages give materially the SAME answer. The extra
  wells buy denser coverage without measurably degrading accuracy, so
  the vintage choice can be made on coverage grounds.

  Distance comparability: LOO is conservative for may1996 and conservative for may2000
  relative to UP villages (section 6g) -- read the error numbers with that
  adjustment in mind.

